-**Pitfalls:**
-*   Reconstructing chairs is more challenging than faces due to the larger search space for pose ([0–360] for azimuth, [0–180] for elevation) and larger variance in geometry, especially thin parts not captured by the shape prior [S2, unofficial].
-*   Finite difference gradient estimation suffers from bias (large spacing) or noise (small spacing), whereas differentiable rendering produces cleaner and unbiased derivative estimations [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Inverse Rendering with Differentiable Ray Tracing](7478-inverse-rendering-with-differentiable-ray-tracing.md)
-Recovers latent scene parameters like camera pose, light placement, and surface color by minimizing photometric error using a differentiable renderer based on the Blinn-Phong model. It specifically estimates the 3D position and intensity of a punctual light source by minimizing the photometric error between a rendered image and an observed RGBD image, using a completely differentiable renderer based on the Blinn-Phong model with cast shadows. Unlike methods that use discrete candidate light sources or explicit shadow detection, this approach performs continuous optimization of light position and renders a shadow map at each iteration. Unlike Neverova et al. [22], this method does not rely on explicit separation between specular and diffuse components, nor does it segment images or extract surface properties.
+Recovers latent scene parameters (camera pose, light placement, surface color) by minimizing photometric error using a differentiable renderer based on the Blinn-Phong model. It specifically estimates the 3D position and intensity of a punctual light source by minimizing photometric error between a rendered image and an observed RGBD image, using continuous optimization of light position and rendering a shadow map at each iteration.
 
 ### [Inverse Rendering with Shape Prior](7506-inverse-rendering-with-shape-prior.md)
-Reconstructs 3D geometry, texture, pose, and lighting from a single image by optimizing a latent representation of a pre-trained 3D auto-encoder decoder, regularizing the reconstruction to prevent noisy results. Unlike [DC-IGN](7478-inverse-rendering-with-differentiable-ray-tracing.md) which learns a disentangled graphics code $Z$ for manipulation, RenderNet explicitly reconstructs 3D geometry, pose, lighting, and texture, allowing for higher resolution (512x512 vs 150x150) and better control in out-of-plane rotation and re-texturing.
+Reconstructs 3D geometry, texture, pose, and lighting from a single image by optimizing a latent representation of a pre-trained 3D auto-encoder decoder, regularizing the reconstruction to prevent noisy results. It explicitly reconstructs 3D geometry, pose, lighting, and texture, allowing for higher resolution and better control in out-of-plane rotation and re-texturing compared to methods that learn a disentangled graphics code.
 
 ### [Differentiable Inverse Graphics Framework](7617-differentiable-inverse-graphics-framework.md)
-A fully differentiable pipeline that infers latent 3D scene parameters—geometry, materials, and lighting—from images by optimizing them via gradient backpropagation from rendering losses. It solves the inverse problem of determining the physical causes of an observed image by minimizing the difference between the observed image and a synthetic render generated by a differentiable renderer. What sets it apart from its siblings is its foundational reliance on the analysis-by-synthesis loop where the rendering equation is explicitly implemented in the forward pass and gradients are propagated through the entire physical simulation (including Monte Carlo path tracing or neural surrogates) to update the parametric scene representation.
+Simultaneously reconstructs 3D geometry, diffuse/specular reflectance, and environment lighting from multi-view photography by minimizing photometric error in a differentiable rendering pipeline. It enforces an energy-conserving image formation process, allowing the estimation of complete continuous HDR light fields from single LDR images while jointly optimizing intrinsic scene properties.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The optimization is iterative and depends on the number of steps required for convergence. Face reconstruction converges in approximately 1800 steps, while chair reconstruction takes around 2000 steps [S2, unofficial]. Each step involves a forward render and a backward pass through the differentiable renderer, which can be computationally expensive depending on the renderer's complexity [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is dominated by the storage of the latent vectors (200-250 dimensions), the intermediate gradients, and the rendered images [S2, unofficial]. Mini-batch gradient descent is used to reduce memory pressure by rendering only a subset of input images per iteration [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The method produces accurate reconstructions when gradients are estimated with low variance. Using Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS) and edge sorting yields reduced variance in gradient estimates, allowing optimization to converge faster and produce more accurate reconstructions compared to using only light or direction sampling without sorting [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** It allows for the joint optimization of complex latent representations (shape, texture, lighting) and handles non-linear relationships between parameters and image appearance through differentiable rendering [S1][S2, unofficial]. The use of shape priors helps regularize the solution space, particularly for objects with high geometric variance [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Reconstructing chairs is more challenging than faces due to the larger search space for pose ([0–360] for azimuth, [0–180] for elevation) and larger variance in geometry, especially thin parts not captured by the shape prior [S2, unofficial]. Convergence is not guaranteed and depends heavily on initialization and the choice of optimization strategy [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Use when the goal is to recover detailed 3D geometry, texture, and lighting from 2D images, especially when a differentiable rendering pipeline is available and computational resources allow for iterative optimization [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Avoid when real-time performance is required, as the optimization process is slow [S2, unofficial]. Also avoid if the object geometry is too complex for the chosen latent representation or shape prior to capture effectively [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of iterations and the cost of gradient estimation. For chair reconstruction, convergence takes around 2000 steps [S2, unofficial]. Performance statistics in [S3, unofficial] show CPU core minutes per iteration ranging from 7.6 to 31 for various scenes.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the resolution of the images and the complexity of the scene representation (e.g., mesh vertices, latent vectors). The latent shape vector dimension is 200-250 [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Produces cleaner and unbiased derivative estimations compared to finite difference methods [S3, unofficial]. Can handle complex scenes with participating media and translucent objects [S3, unofficial]. Shape priors help regularize the solution space for challenging geometries [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Reconstructing objects with large pose search spaces (e.g., chairs with 360-degree azimuth) and high geometric variance (thin parts) is challenging [S2, unofficial]. Requires a differentiable renderer, which can be computationally expensive [S1][S3, unofficial]. May converge to local minima if not initialized properly [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction of geometry, lighting, and texture is required from images, and differentiable rendering infrastructure is available. Suitable for single-image or multi-view reconstruction tasks where explicit ground-truth lighting is not available [S1][S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical, as optimization can be slow [S2, unofficial]. When the object geometry is not well-represented by the chosen prior or parameterization [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from research in differentiable rendering and inverse graphics. Key contributions include the work by Zhang et al. [2020] on estimating interior terms for boundary integrals [S1], and Nicolet et al. [2021] on updating mesh vertex positions using estimated gradients [S1]. The specific application to face and chair reconstruction was detailed in the RenderNet paper [S2, unofficial]. The use of boundary integral techniques for gradient estimation in path-space differentiable rendering was further explored in later work [S1].
+Image-based reconstruction optimization is rooted in the field of inverse rendering, which seeks to infer scene parameters from observed images [S1][S2, S3, unofficial]. The method has been developed in various forms, including face reconstruction [S2, unofficial], chair reconstruction [S2, unofficial], and general inverse rendering with differentiable ray tracing [S1][S3, unofficial]. Key developments include the use of boundary integral techniques for gradient estimation [S1], the integration of deep learning priors via autoencoders [S2, unofficial], and the application of differentiable Monte Carlo path tracing [S3, unofficial]. The method builds on earlier work in differentiable rendering and optimization, such as Zhang et al. [2020] for interior terms and Nicolet et al. [2021] for mesh updates [S1].
 
